zip_view takes any number of views and produces a view of tuples of references to the corresponding elements of the constituent views.

The name views:: zip denotes a customization point object ([customization.point.object]). Given a pack of subexpressions Es..., the expression views:: zip(Es...) is expression-equivalent to auto(views:: empty<tuple<>>) if Es is an empty pack, otherwise, zip_view<views:: all_t<decltype((Es))>...>(Es...). [Example 1:

vector v = {1, 2};
list l = {'a', 'b', 'c'};
auto z = views::zip(v, l);
range_reference_t<decltype(z)> f = z.front(); // f is a tuple<int&, char&>
 // that refers to the first element of v and l
for (auto&& [x, y] : z) {
 cout << '(' << x << ", " << y << ") "; // prints (1, a) (2, b)
}

— end example]

